**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system supporting multi-user operations, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. Users are assigned security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low) and may hold roles, subject to clearance and classification constraints. Requests for actions affect system objects with classification levels, and request submission/processing obeys clearance and FIFO policies. The system ensures correctness, safety, liveness, and consistency under concurrency. The Emergency Module introduces an Emergency mode, with altered access and processing rules, activated and deactivated according to specific policies. Emergency mode is active for a fixed, predetermined duration unless manually ended early by concurrent action of two administrators.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Activation
While operating in Normal mode, exactly two administrators are required to trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Duration and Reversion
Emergency mode is active only for a limited time, defined as a fixed, predetermined duration. However, Emergency mode can be manually ended early by concurrent action of exactly two administrators, after which the system must return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior
During Emergency mode:
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize processing multiple high-classification requests simultaneously.
  R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles.
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges.

R4: Emergency Mode Termination Actions
After Emergency mode ends:
  R4.1: Emergency privileges must be revoked.
  R4.2: Delegated roles during the emergency must be revoked.
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: System actor with a clearance level; may be administrator or non-administrator.
- Administrator: User with administrative privileges; always High clearance.
- Role: Permission set assigned or delegated to users; may have minimum clearance.
- Delegated Role: Role temporarily assigned to a user by a higher-clearance user.
- Clearance Level: Security classification of users/objects (High, Medium, Low).
- System Object: Resource affected by actions, each with a classification level.
- Request: User-submitted action affecting one or more objects, with derived classification.
- Emergency Mode: Special system state with relaxed/altered rules, time-limited; duration is a fixed, predetermined interval, but may end early by the concurrent action of exactly two administrators.
- Emergency Privilege: Temporary privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode.
- Normal Mode: Default operational state.
- Mutual Exclusion: Roles that ordinarily cannot be held simultaneously by a user.
- Emergency Trigger: Action/event by exactly 2 administrators to activate Emergency mode.
- Emergency Deactivation: Action/event by exactly 2 administrators, acting concurrently, to end Emergency mode before the fixed duration elapses.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
None. All previous assumptions regarding Emergency mode duration and reversion have been clarified: Emergency mode lasts for a fixed, predetermined period unless manually ended early through concurrent action by two administrators.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Users may not submit requests with classification higher than their clearance, unless Emergency mode permits it (no such exception is stated, so default policy applies).
- Delegated roles are subject to minimum clearance requirements and may be revoked by administrators at any time.
- Emergency mode can only be activated by exactly two administrators simultaneously while in Normal mode.
- Emergency mode must be time-bounded by a fixed, predetermined duration; system must guarantee eventual reversion to Normal mode. However, Emergency mode may also be manually terminated early by concurrent action of exactly two administrators.
- During Emergency mode, FIFO processing of requests may be bypassed, but only for high-classification requests.
- Temporary mutual exclusion constraints for roles are suspended only during Emergency mode.
- Emergency privileges and delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit from Emergency mode.
- Administrators must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends (nature of "update" to be modeled abstractly as an action/requirement).
